\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The continuous doublet orbit and two finite orbits}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{The matrix of \ref{fdip-00M6} identifies #{SU(2)} with the unit
quaternions, as proved in \ref{fdip-007G}. The normal form in that
proof shows that the first column of a special unitary matrix may
be any unit vector of #{\mathbb C^2}. The orbit of one unit vector
under #{SU(2)} is therefore the full unit sphere. Inside that
sphere, \ref{fdip-0127} gives the orbit of #{24} points under
#{2T} and the orbit of #{120} points under #{2I}.}
